BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Botanical Garden": "https://torontobotanicalgarden.ca/event/petal-to-paper-paper-cherry-blossom-workshop/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:toronto_botanical
X-SOURCE:Toronto Botanical Garden
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260414T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260414T143000
DTSTAMP:20260316T021437
CREATED:20260226T142035Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260226T142257Z
UID:10001941-1776160800-1776177000@torontobotanicalgarden.ca
SUMMARY:Petal to Paper: Paper Cherry Blossom Workshop
DESCRIPTION:Join us to create lifelike paper cherry blossoms with botanical accuracy!Beautiful Cherry Blossoms arrive in early Spring but to our dismay the blooms are so short. Join paper artist Nancy Lalicon for the “Paper to Petal: Cherry Blossoms Workshop” to create your own bouquet of flowers which will last much\, much longer. In this 4-hour session\, participants will learn to create lifelike Cherry Blossoms in two shades of pink. Nancy will guide you through constructing the flowers using precut petals and leaf templates. You will have the opportunity to make as many branches as you wish. Discover the art of paper floristry and leave with your own stunning creations. Optional Items To Bring: Vase to keep your floral creation inSharp scissors (4”-5”)Preferred tools such as long nose pliers or wire cuttersAdditional colouring materialsTuesday\, April 14 from 10 a.m. to 2:30 p.m. There will be a 30-minute break for lunch. We recommend bringing a lunch as there are limited options nearby. Public $67\, TBG Members $57 (plus applicable taxes and fees) About Nancy Lalicon Nancy Lalicon is a multi-media sculptor specialized in making botanically accurate paper flowers and plants\, and in facilitating workshops for for community art organizations and local groups around Toronto and the GTA. From Peonies to Jupiter’s Beard to the Blazing Star\, Nancy has mastered the intricate art of creating environmentally friendly paper replicas; a modern day\, three dimensional take on botanical drawing. Trained in the Fine Arts at the University of Victoria and the Emily Carr College of Art in BC\, she received an MFA from Concordia University\, Montreal. Nancy lives in East Toronto with a large family who lovingly tolerate all her studio hours and mountains of flowers and roots.  Join Toronto Botanical GardenJoin the Toronto Botanical Garden (TBG) as a member to get a discount on this program\, upcoming events and classes\, and free parking. A TBG membership helps you deepen your knowledge and connection to the natural world\, become a part of a dynamic community of plant people\, and be inspired! Become a member!  Additional Program InformationPlease review our Refund Policy and Terms of Agreement.For more information on accessibility at the garden please review our Accessibility Features webpage. If you have any accessibility requirements during your program please email programsupport@torontobotanicalgarden.ca.To learn more about the Toronto Botanical Garden and our programs\, please review our list of Frequently Asked Questions.
URL:https://torontobotanicalgarden.ca/event/petal-to-paper-paper-cherry-blossom-workshop/
LOCATION:Toronto Botanical Garden\, 777 Lawrence Avenue East\, Toronto\, ON\, M3C 1P2\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Art
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://torontobotanicalgarden.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/88b45421501d9923e67e701ba9906675.jpg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"UofT Engineering": "https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/robotics-institute-seminar-tobi-fischer-sensing-change-brain-inspired-navigation-beyond-gps/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:uoft_engineering
X-SOURCE:UofT Engineering
DTSTART;TZID=UTC:20260414T150000
DTEND;TZID=UTC:20260414T160000
DTSTAMP:20260313T214425
CREATED:20260312T223945Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260312T223945Z
UID:10004352-1776178800-1776182400@www.engineering.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Robotics Institute Seminar: Tobi Fischer\, “Sensing Change: Brain-Inspired Navigation Beyond GPS”
DESCRIPTION:Sensing Change: Brain-Inspired Navigation Beyond GPS \nJoin in MY580 or Zoom \nAbstract\n\nKnowing your location is fundamental to navigation – for humans\, animals\, and autonomous systems alike. But how can robots and other autonomous agents determine their position with confidence and precision in environments where satellite systems are unavailable or unreliable? The central theme of this talk explores Visual Place Recognition (VPR)\, which is the ability to recognise previously visited locations using only visual data. I will demonstrate how energy-efficient approaches using bio-inspired event-based cameras and spiking neural networks can provide low-power edge devices with location information with superior energy efficiency\, adaptability\, and data efficiency. Beyond this central theme\, I will also touch on my work on underwater perception for reef restoration\, and about Pixi\, a powerful tool to run many robotics\, computer vision\, and AI libraries on any operating system (Linux\, MacOS\, Windows\, or even your browser!).\n\nSpeaker Bio\n\nDr Tobias Fischer is a Senior Lecturer (US: Associate Professor) and ARC DECRA Fellow at the Queensland University of Technology (QUT)\, where he conducts research at the intersection of neuromorphic computing\, computer vision\, and robotics. He conducts research in robot localisation and underwater perception\, blending neuroscience and robotics to push the boundaries of intelligent systems operating under resource constraints. Dr Fischer has attracted over $3 million in competitive funding and leads research within the QUT Centre for Robotics and the Reef Restoration and Adaptation Program\, with previous grants from Intel and Amazon. He obtained his PhD from Imperial College London\, where his thesis won the UK Best PhD in Robotics Award. He has published more than 50 papers in top venues including Science Robotics\, IEEE Transactions on Robotics\, IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence\, CVPR\, ICCV\, ECCV\, ICRA\, and IROS. Dr Fischer serves as Associate Editor for leading robotics journals and conferences and co-chairs the IEEE Robotics and Automation Society Women in Engineering Committee. His PhD students have gone on to successful careers as Assistant Professor\, Research Scientists\, and Research Fellows. Website: https://www.tobiasfischer.info
URL:https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/robotics-institute-seminar-tobi-fischer-sensing-change-brain-inspired-navigation-beyond-gps/
LOCATION:Myhal Centre for Engineering Innovation & Entrepreneurship\, Room 580\, or on Zoom\, 55 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5S 0C9\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Lectures, seminars and workshops
END:VEVENT
